Coulomb's law can be used to define an electrostatic unit of charge so that the numerical value of the coefficient $k$ in formula (8-2) is 1. **For two equal point charges in vacuum, separated by 1 centimeter and interacting with a force of magnitude 1 dyne ($10^{-5}$ newton), the magnitude of each charge is defined as one absolute electrostatic unit.**
This convention specifies the units of length, force, and charge together;
it does not arbitrarily remove a coefficient from formulas expressed in other systems of units.

The seventh-grade textbook mentioned in the original source described the method then used to establish the ampere standard from forces between current-carrying wires.
The present SI instead defines the ampere through the fixed numerical value of the elementary charge and the second;
the historical method must not be presented as today's definition.

When current varies with time, the charge accumulated over the interval must be calculated;
a current value at just one instant is insufficient.
With the approximate conversion used in this lesson, one coulomb is about $3\times10^9$ absolute electrostatic units:

The direction is determined separately: charges of the same sign repel, and charges of opposite signs attract.
This formula applies to stationary charges in vacuum that can be treated as point charges;
it cannot be applied without qualification to macroscopic media or arbitrary charge distributions.

